<title>USA: US Feb import prices -0.7 pct, export prices +0.1.</title>
<headline>US Feb import prices -0.7 pct, export prices +0.1.</headline>
<dateline>WASHINGTON 1997-03-21</dateline>
<text>
<p>Labor Department report of</p>
<p>U.S. import and export price changes by percent (unadjusted).</p>
<p>    -			    Feb    Jan-Rev from  Feb 96/95</p>
<p> ALL IMPORTS		  -0.7   -0.2   -0.3	0.6</p>
<p> -Petroleum		   -7.2    1.4    1.3     21.9</p>
<p> -Nonpetroleum		 0.1   -0.4   -0.4     -1.7</p>
<p> -Food, Feed, Drink	  1.8   UNCH   -0.3     -0.2</p>
<p> -Industrial Supplies     -2.2    1.2    1.2	7.6</p>
<p> -Capital Goods	     -0.6   -1.7   -1.8     -5.6</p>
<p> -Motor Vehicles,Parts    UNCH   -0.1   -0.1     UNCH</p>
<p> -Consumer Goods ExAutos   0.2   -0.1   -0.1     -0.5</p>
<p>Percent changes, not seasonally adjusted --</p>
<p> ALL EXPORTS		   0.1    0.2    0.2     -1.2</p>
<p> -Agricultural		 2.2    0.1    0.1     -6.2</p>
<p> -Non-Agricultural	  -0.1    0.1    0.1     -0.4</p>
<p> -Food, Feed, Drink	  2.1   -0.3   -0.3     -6.9</p>
<p> -Industrial Supplies	0.1    0.2    0.2     -1.4</p>
<p> -Capital Goods	     -0.1    0.2    0.2     -0.2</p>
<p> -Motor Vehicles,Parts    UNCH    0.2    0.2	0.7</p>
<p> -Consumer Goods ExAutos  UNCH    0.1    0.1	0.9</p>
<p>The department reported February price indexes (1990 base year equals 100) of 101.0 for imports, and 99.6 for exports.</p>
<p>The 7.2 pct drop in petroleum import prices in February was the biggest since September 1994, when they fell 7.5 percent.</p>
</text>
